Since all copies of the target are next to each other, one scan from the left can find the first copy and one scan from the right can find the last copy. The loops stop as soon as they find a match.

```go
func searchRange(nums []int, target int) []int {
	leftIndex := -1
	for index := 0; index < len(nums); index++ {
		if nums[index] == target {
			leftIndex = index
			break
		}
	}

	if leftIndex == -1 {
		return []int{-1, -1}
	}

	rightIndex := -1
	for index := len(nums) - 1; index >= 0; index-- {
		if nums[index] == target {
			rightIndex = index
			break
		}
	}

	return []int{leftIndex, rightIndex}
}
```

For `[1, 3, 3, 3, 8]` and target `3`, the left loop stops at `1` and the right loop stops at `3`. An empty slice or a missing target returns `[-1, -1]`.

This avoids recursion and is easy to follow, but it does not take advantage of binary search, so a large input can require two long scans.

Time complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

The sorted order lets `lower_bound` find the first position where the target can appear. `upper_bound` gives the position after the final target, so subtracting one gives the last index.

If the target is not present, the first iterator may point to the end or to another value.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
public:
    vector<int> searchRange(vector<int> &nums, int target) {
        auto first = lower_bound(nums.begin(), nums.end(), target);
        if (first == nums.end() || *first != target) {
            return {-1, -1};
        }

        auto afterLast = upper_bound(nums.begin(), nums.end(), target);
        int left = static_cast<int>(first - nums.begin());
        int right = static_cast<int>(afterLast - nums.begin()) - 1;
        return {left, right};
    }
};
```

For example, in `[1, 2, 2, 2, 5]`, the two searches point to indexes `1` and `4`, so the answer is `[1, 3]`.

Time complexity: `O(log n)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

A normal binary search can find any copy of the target first. After that, the equal values form one continuous block because the array is sorted. Moving left and right from the found copy gives both ends.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] searchRange(int[] nums, int target) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = nums.length - 1;
        int found = -1;

        while (left <= right) {
            int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;

            if (nums[middle] == target) {
                found = middle;
                break;
            } else if (nums[middle] < target) {
                left = middle + 1;
            } else {
                right = middle - 1;
            }
        }

        if (found == -1) {
            return new int[]{-1, -1};
        }

        int first = found;
        while (first > 0 && nums[first - 1] == target) {
            first--;
        }

        int last = found;
        while (last + 1 < nums.length && nums[last + 1] == target) {
            last++;
        }

        return new int[]{first, last};
    }
}
```

The binary search is fast, but the two expanding loops can visit many equal values. For an array made entirely of the target, the total time becomes `O(n)`.

Time complexity: `O(log n + k)`, where `k` is the number of matching values, and `O(n)` in the largest case  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

Two recursive searches are used here. One keeps moving left after finding the target, and the other keeps moving right. The final matching indexes are saved when no better position exists on that side.

```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
    int findFirst(const vector<int>& nums, int target, int left, int right) {
        if (left > right) {
            return -1;
        }

        int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
        if (nums[middle] == target) {
            int earlier = findFirst(nums, target, left, middle - 1);
            return earlier == -1 ? middle : earlier;
        }

        if (nums[middle] < target) {
            return findFirst(nums, target, middle + 1, right);
        }
        return findFirst(nums, target, left, middle - 1);
    }

    int findLast(const vector<int>& nums, int target, int left, int right) {
        if (left > right) {
            return -1;
        }

        int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
        if (nums[middle] == target) {
            int later = findLast(nums, target, middle + 1, right);
            return later == -1 ? middle : later;
        }

        if (nums[middle] < target) {
            return findLast(nums, target, middle + 1, right);
        }
        return findLast(nums, target, left, middle - 1);
    }

public:
    vector<int> searchRange(vector<int> &nums, int target) {
        if (nums.empty()) {
            return {-1, -1};
        }

        int first = findFirst(nums, target, 0, static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1);
        if (first == -1) {
            return {-1, -1};
        }

        int last = findLast(nums, target, 0, static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1);
        return {first, last};
    }
};
```

The empty array is handled before starting recursion. The input vector is only read, so it is not changed.

Time complexity: `O(log n)`  
Space complexity: `O(log n)` for the recursive call stack

